  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1237 PM CDT Thu Jun 11 2026\n\nThe cold front has made its way through the CWA bringing strong \nnortherly winds to the area. These winds should taper off as the \nafternoon progresses. Winds then become light this evening with the \nwind direction shifting to the south by tomorrow morning. Southerly \nwinds continue through tomorrow and become breezy across the western \nhalf of the forecast area. Skies will be mostly clear through this \nevening with increasing mid to upper level cloudiness along the \nKS/OK border after midnight into tomorrow. There could be an \nisolated shower along the KS/OK border tomorrow morning but most \nplaces look to remain dry. Short range models depict thunderstorms \nto develop across eastern Colorado tomorrow afternoon and slide \nalong the KS/OK border tomorrow night.  SPC currently doesn't show \nany chances of these storms to become severe, but I wouldn't be \nsurprised if they place a marginal risk in future outlooks. As for \ntemperatures, Highs this afternoon look to range from around 80 \ndegrees across west central Kansas to low 90s across south central \nKansas. Lows tonight are expected to range from the low 50s across \nwest central Kansas to low 60s across south central Kansas. Highs \ntomorrow are progged to reach into the upper 80s with lows tomorrow \nnight ranging from the lower 60s across west central Kansas to \naround 70 degrees across south central Kansas. \n\nExpect a very warm day on Saturday as a warm front moves through the \narea. Highs in the 90s are expected with around 100 degrees along \nthe KS/OK border. Winds will be out of the south in the morning but \nthis will be short lived as a strong cold front comes sweeping \nthrough the area in the afternoon shifting winds to the north. \nTHunderstorms are expected to form along this front in the afternoon \nand evening hours. Any storms that do develop will be capable of \nproducing large hail and damaging winds.  Currently I believe most \nof this activity will be south and east of our area but have kept \nthe NBM POPs and thunderstorms in the WX grids for now. Thunderstorm \nchances continue throughout the remainder of the weekend into early \nnext week but it will be contingent on the evolution of previous \nstorms if we actually receive any precipitation. A drier pattern \nemerges by mid next week as ridging builds over the western United \nStates. As for temperatures, expect below normal temperatures Sunday \nand Monday with high only reaching into the 70s. We then warm up \ninto the 80s Tuesday with 90s on Wednesday and Thursday. Lows will \ngenerally be in the 50s Sunday through Tuesday morning with 60s \nreturning thereafter. Low 70s will be possible across south central \nKansas mid to late next week.",
